[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Fetching Strategies: Lazy vs Eager Loading and N+1 Problem in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building enterprise Java applications using JPA and Hibernate, managing how related entities are retrieved from the database is crucial for performance. Choosing the wrong fetching strategy can lead to severe performance bottlenecks, most notably the dreaded N+1 query problem."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Fetching Strategies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"JPA provides two primary fetching strategies to handle entity relationships: Eager Loading and Lazy Loading. These are defined using the fetch attribute on annotations like @ManyToOne, @OneToMany, @OneToOne, and @ManyToMany."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Eager Loading: Associated entities are fetched immediately along with the parent entity using a JOIN query. This is the default strategy for @ManyToOne and @OneToOne relationships."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lazy Loading: Associated entities are not fetched from the database until you explicitly invoke their getter methods. This is the default strategy for @OneToMany and @ManyToMany relationships."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Defining Fetch Types"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is an example demonstrating both lazy and eager configurations in a JPA Entity model."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class Department {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    // Lazy loading is default for OneToMany, but can be specified explicitly
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Employee> employees;
}

@Entity
public class Employee {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    // Eager loading forces Hibernate to fetch the Department immediately
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
    private Department department;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"The N+1 Query Problem Explained"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The N+1 problem typically occurs when using Lazy Loading incorrectly. Suppose you fetch a list of N departments using a single query (1 query). Then, you iterate through the departments and access their lazy-loaded employees. Hibernate will execute N additional queries to fetch the employees for each department, resulting in 1 + N total database round-trips."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// This loop triggers the N+1 problem if employees are Lazy loaded
List<Department> departments = entityManager.createQuery("SELECT d FROM Department d", Department.class).getResultList();

for (Department dept : departments) {
    // Triggers a separate SQL query for EACH department
    System.out.println(dept.getEmployees().size());
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"How to Solve the N+1 Problem"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To avoid the N+1 query problem, you should instruct Hibernate to fetch the related entities in a single query using JOIN FETCH or Entity Graphs."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JPQL JOIN FETCH: Explicitly tell the persistence provider to join and load the associations in the initial query."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Entity Graphs: Use @NamedEntityGraph to dynamically define which attributes should be fetched eagerly for a specific query."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Solving N+1 using JPQL JOIN FETCH
List<Department> departments = entityManager.createQuery(
    "SELECT DISTINCT d FROM Department d LEFT JOIN FETCH d.employees", 
    Department.class
).getResultList();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default to Lazy Loading: Keep collections as LAZY to avoid pulling unnecessary data into memory."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use JOIN FETCH in queries: Fetch associations upfront in your JPQL/Criteria queries only when you know you will need them."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor SQL logs: Enable show-sql in your application properties during development to catch accidental N+1 queries early."}]
